# TUI-DESIGN-5 §2.3 / §2.14 / §10 (slot R5-1): `/who` under a real pty — the row ladder at 80×24, then the
# 40-column rung, then `--all`, then `/peers`. Hermetic: no network, no second process.
#
# GAP 1 UPDATE (`openCoordination()` / the read half pinned to the writer's handle), plus the round-5 fix pass.
# Four facts about this file changed and are written here rather than left to be rediscovered:
#
#   - **The rows are real now, and it is MEASURED.** Before gap 1 closed, step 1 answered `who · unknown / the
#     session ledger is not open yet` and every later step measured nothing: the write half beat, but no reader
#     was wired to the handle. Step 0's `--mock` run opens the ledger, and step 1 now renders THIS session's own
#     row off the live fold. `sh test/pty/run-smoke.sh r5-who r5-who-ascii` → **both PASS, exit 0, timeouts=0**
#     (`who · 1 live, 0 gone` / `who - 1 live, 0 gone`), with `beat-no-key-bytes` and `beat-no-long-hostkey`.
#     Run `node scripts/build.mjs` first: this script drives `bin/jevcode.js`, which loads `dist/jevcode.mjs`, so
#     a stale bundle measures the previous commit.
#   - **Step 4 used to hard-time-out, and the fix is in `/peers`, not here.** With the ledger open `host.peers()`
#     returns a real `PeerView` whose counts are 0, so `/peers` took the empty-state branch — which was the one
#     §12.1 block head in the file with no ` · ` in it, and `expect peers [·-]` could therefore never match
#     (exit 124, timeouts=1, pre-existing at the branch tip). The empty state now carries the dot like every
#     other head (`peers · 0 here, 0 stale`) and the sentence is its body (fix pass, finding 5).
#   - **`JEVCODE_FAULT=peer:5` is GONE from the env row** (fix pass, finding 19). `src/tui/faults.ts` parses and
#     validates `peer:<n>` and nothing in the tree reads it (`grep -rn "kind === 'peer'" src/` is empty), so a
#     reader of `run-smoke.sh` was being told the rows came from an injected fault. They come from step 0's run.
#   - **Two sessions seeing each other is proved in vitest, not here.** A `.steps` file cannot spawn the second
#     process or seed a fixture before the child starts, and the peer's `wsKey` has to be this scenario's own
#     temp workspace or the two never meet. `test/pty/round5.pty.test.ts`'s `r5-who-peer` case does it with
#     `Scenario.during`, spawns a real second `jevcode run … --plain --mock` under the SAME `JEVCODE_HOME` and
#     the same workspace, and asserts `who · 2 live` plus `assertNoKeyBytes` over both sessions' subtrees. Its
#     `--ascii` / 40-column twin (`r5-who-peer-ascii40`) lives beside it, so the only capture of REAL peer rows
#     is no longer a single geometry.
#
# TWO scenarios share this file (the `--ascii` twin cannot be a resize — the glyph set is chosen at launch):
#   r5-who        chat --mock            the unicode rows (`●`, `◌`, `○`, `·`)
#   r5-who-ascii  chat --mock --ascii    the same rows with `*`, `.`, `o`, `-` (§12.1 S1–S3, S10)
#
# What it proves, beyond "it renders":
#   - the §2.3 column ladder drops right to left on a resize and never truncates silently (§7 row 85);
#   - a crashed row and a bench row are DIFFERENT row shapes, not variants (§7 rows 3 and 12) — both need a
#     second writer and are therefore exercised by the fold fixtures of `test/unit/session/peers.test.ts`, not
#     by this capture;
#   - §2.14: step 1 runs a real `--mock` task FIRST, so the write half actually opens the ledger, mints the claim
#     and starts the heartbeat writer before anything is read — the beat file below exists because of it;
#   - gate G-R5-9 / §7 row 61 / §2.14 consequence 3: `assertNoKeyBytes` runs over the frames AND over the
#     coordination tree this session wrote (the writer is the only thing that can put device key material near a
#     file), and over the `run.lock`, which round 5 widened with a `bootAt`.
#
# run-smoke.sh needs FIVE rows for these two scenarios; `test/pty/**` is R5-2's (§9.2, W5), so they are a request
# in R5-1's report, verbatim:
#   alias  : r5-who-ascii) steps_name=r5-who;;
#   env    : r5-who|r5-who-ascii) extra_env="$extra_env JEVCODE_ASSERT_NO_NETWORK=1";;
#   check  : r5-who|r5-who-ascii) grep -q 'who [·-] ' "$txt" && checks="$checks r5-who:block" || { ok=0; checks="$checks MISSING:r5-who-block"; }
#   keys   : r5-who|r5-who-ascii) if [ -d "$home/coordination" ]; then grep -rqI "$FAKE_KEY" "$home/coordination" && { ok=0; checks="$checks LEAK:beat-key-bytes"; } || checks="$checks beat-no-key-bytes"; grep -rqIE '"hostKey"[^,}]*[0-9a-f]{32}' "$home/coordination" && { ok=0; checks="$checks LEAK:beat-hostkey"; } || checks="$checks beat-no-long-hostkey"; else checks="$checks beat-absent"; fi;;
#   select : sel_named r5-who && run r5-who 0 24 80 chat --mock
#            sel_named r5-who-ascii && run r5-who-ascii 0 24 80 chat --mock --ascii
expect \x1b\[\?25l
expect Say hi
sleep 0.4

# 0 — §2.14: a real run, so the WRITE half runs (ledger open → claim mint → heartbeat writer), all post-first-frame
send fix the failing test
sleep 0.2
send \r
expect run
sleep 1.2
mark ran

# 1 — the full-width block at 80×24: the head, then one row per session
send /who
sleep 0.2
send \r
expect who [·-]
sleep 0.4
mark who-80

# 2 — the 40-column rung: the same rows, three cells each (`● <label>  step n/m <stage>  beat <age>`)
resize 24 40
sleep 0.8
mark who-40

# 3 — back to 80 and the `--all` form (stale > 10 min and ignored-device rows join, and S9 appears only here)
resize 24 80
sleep 0.6
send /who --all
sleep 0.2
send \r
sleep 0.4
mark who-all

# 4 — `/peers` keeps TD4's four-scalar head beside `/who` (D-AC (b): two commands, two questions); §2.4's kv rows
#     are superseded, so the block is the head, the counts and the ` · /who shows what each is doing` pointer
send /peers
sleep 0.2
send \r
expect peers [·-]
sleep 0.3
mark peers

send /exit
sleep 0.2
send \r
eof
